<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tepanecs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tepanec</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tepanecs were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> whose state, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>, dominated the region in the early fifteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> initially served them as tributaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> warriors. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> describe how the Tepanec succession crisis after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>'s death in 1426 led to the war in which the Mexica and their allies destroyed Tepanec hegemony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tepanecs.jpg" alt="Tepanecs" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glifo_gentilicio_tepaneca_codice_azcatitlan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
